{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_241157139#8_545157995","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives a direct answer—Marius reformed the Roman army by recruiting landless citizens and reorganizing legions into cohorts—but the source is a broad list of famous Roman generals rather than a document dedicated to Marius' change or the Marian reforms.","learned":"Repeated broad-list evidence says Marius authorized recruitment of landless citizens and reorganized the Roman legions into cohorts."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ marius poor citizens join \"roman army\")","reason":"Search for focused documents explaining the core reform in simpler terms: Marius allowed poor or landless citizens to join the Roman army."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ marius poor citizens join \"roman army\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_1274836271#3_2684536018","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1863","rank":1,"snippet":"Ancient Roman History Was Written by Upper-Class Men Ancient Egypt and the History of Pyramids Ancient Roman Virtues and Power \" , \"segment\": \"Image: Morphart Creation/Shutterstock) Very rarely, the Roman authorities do seem to have acknowledged the plight of the poor and sought to do something about it. In 107 B.C., Roman General Gaius Marius permitted <cover>citizens who did not own any land and were excluded from service in the army to join as volunteers for his campaign against Jugurtha in North Africa. And the poor population volunteered in large numbers in order to escape destitution. However, it must be mentioned here that Marius was not a social reformer, he didn’t really care about the poor. He merely wanted to solve a manpower crisis in the Roman Army. </cover>Learn more about being a Roman soldier. Occupation of the Poor in Ancient Rome The poor people generally had to work as unskilled workers, getting themselves hired on a daily basis to perform a variety of menial jobs. They were known as a mercenarius —the modern equivalent word being ‘mercenary’—meaning a person who works for money. The decent folk despised them because, like "}}
