{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_362955411#11_336506760","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly supports the answer by stating that after Marius's reforms there were no property requirements and even the poorest Roman citizens could join the legions, but the document is broadly about legionaries rather than dedicated to Marius or the Marian reforms.","learned":"Under Marius's reforms, state-provided equipment and elimination of property requirements allowed the poorest Roman citizens to join the legions."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"marian reforms\" (+ landless poor property) \"roman army\")","reason":"Search for documents explicitly about the Marian reforms and the key recruitment/property change, with broader variants than the failed exact-property queries."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"marian reforms\" (+ landless poor property) \"roman army\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3718936904#2_3819994898","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1865","rank":1,"snippet":"increasing invasions and uprisings across Roman territory had stretched the human and physical resources of the Roman army. The ' maniple ' militia (temporary service) used since the Samnite Wars (343-290BC) emerged as inadequate for the demands of Rome's expanding territory. Marius proposed radical alterations with the intention of creating a more professional, permanent and dynamic <cover>Roman army. The reforms revolutionized the Roman military machine, introducing the standardized legionary, the cohort unit and drastically altered the property and weaponry requirements for recruitment. As a consequence these reforms had a significant impact on the military supremacy of Rome, as well as unintentionally contributing to the social and political disruption of the Late Republic. These changes remained principally intact until the downfall of the Roman Empire, but there were structural and administrative adjustments, notably by Augustus and later by Diocletian. Contents 1 Background 2 Marian reforms</cover>3 Impact of Marian reforms 4 Subsequent modifications 5 Further reading 6 References 7 External links Background The pre-Marius Roman army was organised as a conscript levy of all male citizens, similar to other Poleis especially in Greece. It was considered the duty of every physical able man to serve in the army when a "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"12/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3718936904#2_3819994898","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to the Marian reforms and directly states that Marius created a more professional, permanent Roman army, introduced standardized legionaries and cohort units, and drastically altered property and weaponry requirements for recruitment.","learned":"Marius' reform changed the Roman army into a more professional, permanent force with standardized legionaries/cohorts and changed recruitment property and equipment requirements."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1865","reason":"This Marian reforms query is highly focused and produced a grade-3 result; continue to find more documents or segments with direct evidence about the specific changes."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1865"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_1432636283#3_2192084847","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1865","rank":3,"snippet":": \"After the Punic wars, the Roman state got way too big for the antiquated Republican institutions to govern. The senate governed provinces through appointed proconsuls, who held dictatorial power within their designated provinces, and this method of government proved to be ineffective. Professionalization of the army. During the early and mid republic, roman soldiers were citizen farmers, who fought for the state as their civic duty. In addition, all the conquered areas had to provide a certain number of troops for the <cover>roman army as a part of their alliance with the roman state. These soldiers were loyal to the state. With Marian reforms, the job of a soldier became paid and landless </cover>poor were given the opportunity to enlist. The weird quirk of this system was that the state didn’t actually pay the soldiers, the soldiers were paid by their general, and much of the pay was war booty, so the soldiers were loyal to their general rather than the state. These generals then became more powerful than the senate, because they had armies and the senate didn’t. Civil wars between Rome’s generals were bound to lead to an eventual winner, who would take ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3718936904#1_3819992011","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"13/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_50_1432636283#3_2192084847","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly explains the Marian reform as professionalizing the army by making soldiering a paid job and allowing the landless poor to enlist, but the document appears to be a broader discussion of Roman political change rather than dedicated to Marius' reform itself.","learned":"Marian reforms professionalized soldiering, opened enlistment to the landless poor, and shifted soldiers' loyalty toward generals."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1865","reason":"The focused Marian reforms query is still returning direct evidence; continue to look for more dedicated sources explaining the property/recruitment and professional-army changes."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1865"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3718936904#3_3819997040","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1865","rank":4,"snippet":"would then be charged with the duty of recruiting an army from the eligible citizenry of the Republic, which would have to undergo intensive training until they could be truly considered an army. Armies would then gain experiences during their campaigning, until they were disbanded with the end of the war. The soldiers would leave his farm or business and serve for the duration of a campaign/war, and then be released back into the populace. Soldiers were paid very low, if any, wages and the delays of this money were notoriously common. The soldiers were mostly compensated by a share of the loot. To be eligible to serve in this <cover>Roman army the citizens had to satisfy stringent property and census requirements, as well as providing their own weaponry, supplies and armour. This system of citizen-soldiers who brought their own equipment in at least comparable quality, worked well as long as the wars were relatively short affairs fought relatively close to home.\" , \"start_char\": 2902.000000 , \"title\": \"Marian reforms - </cover>Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marian_reforms\" }"}}
