{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1867"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_429362754#13_754396419","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1867","rank":4,"snippet":"\"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_429362754#13_754396419\" , \"end_char\": 8685.000000 , \"headings\": \"FC31: The Fall of the Roman Republic (133-31 BCE) FC31: The Fall of the Roman Republic (133-31 BCE) Pattern of decline First attempts at reform: the Gracchi Marius and the Roman army Sulla and the First Civil War The rise of Julius Caesar Octavian, Antony, and two more civil wars (44-31 B.C.E.) \" , \"segment\": \"However, <cover>Marius' recruiting and tactical reforms created a much more efficient and professional army, which is what Rome </cover>needed at this time. In Marius' sixth consulship, the Cimbri and Teutones finally got around to invading Italy after a leisurely rampage through Spain and Gaul. The newly reformed legions cleverly maneuvered the invaders into a bad position and then destroyed them under the hot Italian sun. Marius was the hero of the hour and acclaimed the Third Founder of Rome after the legendary Romulus and Camillus. Marius may have been a good general, but he was a mediocre politician. When his ally, the tribune Saturninus, tried to seize power, a riot "}}
