{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1705020336#6_1921678472","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly supports the key answer by saying the Roman army property requirement was finally scrapped in the consulship of Gaius Marius in 107 BC, but the document is broadly about the mid-Republic Roman army rather than dedicated to Marius' reforms.","learned":"Marius' recruitment change can be stated as scrapping the Roman army property requirement in 107 BC."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"gaius marius\" reforms poor army)","reason":"Search a broader full-name formulation likely to retrieve additional focused documents explaining that Marius' reforms opened the army to poor citizens and changed the Roman military."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"gaius marius\" reforms poor army)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1174894755#0_1328771963","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1874","rank":1,"snippet":"post of Consul. Two were elected each year to head the government of the state, and would be assigned a consular army and an area in which to campaign. From <cover>Gaius Marius and Sulla onwards, control of the army began to be tied into the political ambitions of individuals, leading to the first triumvirate of the 1st century BC and the resulting Caesar’s civil war. The late Republic and Empire was increasingly plagued by usurpations led by or supported by the military, leading to the crisis of the third century in the late empire. Contents 1 Roman kingdom 2 Roman Republic 3 From late Republic to mid-Roman Empire 4 Middle Roman Empire 5 Late Roman Empire 6 Political economy of the Roman military 7 References Roman kingdom Under the Etruscan king Servius Tullius, a person's social standing and wealth determined both their political and military role: following his reforms, a rich man would have had greater voting rights, and greater standing within the military, than a poor </cover>man.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Political history of the Roman military - Wikipedia\" , \"url\""}}
