{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ marian military reforms recruitment army)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_2449758203#11_2769113823","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1878","rank":4,"snippet":"end_char\": 10000.000000 , \"headings\": \"Structural history of the Roman military Structural history of the Roman <cover>military Phase I Phase II Phase III Phase IV Contents Early Roman army Tribal forces (c. 752 BC – c. 578 BC) Etruscan-model hoplites (578 BC – c. 315 BC) Professionalisation during the Republican period Manipular legion (315–107 BC) Proletarianisation of the infantry (217–107 BC) Marian legion (107–27 BC) Non-citizen recruitment (49–27 BC) The army at the height of the Empire Imperial legions and reformation of the auxilia (27 BC – 117 AD) Introduction of vexillationes (76–117 AD) The army during the decline of the Empire Barbarisation of the army (117–253 AD) Successive crises (238–359 AD) Comitatenses and limitanei (284–395 AD) Adoption of barbarian allies (358–395 AD) Collapse in the West and survival in the East (395–476 AD) References Explanatory notes Citations Bibliography Primary sources Secondary and tertiary sources Further reading \" , \"segment\": \"During the reforms, </cover>this crude division of poorer and richer citizens was further stratified. The army thereafter consisted of a number of troop types based upon the social ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3718936904#2_3819994898","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3718936904#1_3819992011","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_890921568#2_1034941911","rank":3,"relevance":1}]}}
