{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_03_1477560347#7_2441145572","relevance":2,"reason":"This passage directly supports the answer by saying the Marian reforms produced a professional standing army, but it is a broad Roman Army article rather than a document dedicated to Marius' change.","learned":"The Marian reforms are summarized as creating a professional standing army for Rome, with later loyalty often shifting toward individual generals."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ marius changed \"roman army\")","reason":"Search a simple direct formulation likely to retrieve explanatory pages focused on how Marius changed the Roman army, without over-constraining terminology."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ marius changed \"roman army\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_424243675#3_953188535","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1881","rank":1,"snippet":"\": \"Antiquity's Best Generals and Commanders Antiquity's Best Generals and Commanders Alexander the Great, Conqueror of Most of the Known World Attila the Hun, the Scourge of God Hannibal, Who Almost Conquered Rome Julius Caesar, Conqueror of Gaul Marius, Reformer of the Roman Army Alaric the Visigoth, Who Sacked Rome Cyrus the Great, Founder of the Persian Empire Scipio Africanus, Who Beat Hannibal Sun Tzu, Author of \\\"The Art of War\\\" Trajan, Who Expanded the Roman Empire \" , \"segment\": \"Marius, Reformer of the <cover>Roman Army DIREKTOR / Wikipedia / Public Domain Marius needed more troops, so he instituted policies that changed </cover>the complexion of the Roman army and most armies after that. Instead of requiring a minimum property qualification of his soldiers, Marius recruited poor soldiers with promises of pay and land. To serve as a military leader against Rome's enemies, Marius was elected consul a record-breaking seven times. Alaric the Visigoth, Who Sacked Rome Charles Phelps Cushing / ClassicStock / Getty Images The Visigoth king Alaric was told he would conquer Rome, but his troops treated the imperial capital with notable tenderness—they spared Christian churches, thousands of souls who sought refuge "}}
