{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_424243675#3_953188535","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly says Marius changed the Roman army by ending the minimum property qualification and recruiting poor soldiers with promises of pay and land, but the document is a broad list of ancient generals rather than dedicated to Marius' reforms.","learned":"Marius changed the Roman army by recruiting poor soldiers instead of requiring a minimum property qualification, offering pay and land."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ marius \"minimum property qualification\" soldiers)","reason":"Search the exact evidence-bearing phrase from the latest result to find more focused documents on Marius ending property qualifications for Roman army service."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ marius \"minimum property qualification\" soldiers)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_424243675#2_953186200","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1882","rank":1,"snippet":"Images Julius Caesar not only led the army and won many battles, but he wrote about his military adventures. It's from his description of the wars of the Romans against the Gauls (in modern France) that we get the familiar line Gallia est omnis divisa in partes tres: \" All Gaul is divided into three parts,\" which Caesar proceeded to conquer. Marius, Reformer of the Roman Army DIREKTOR / Wikipedia / Public Domain Marius needed more troops, so he instituted policies that changed the complexion of the Roman army and most armies after that. Instead of requiring a <cover>minimum property qualification of his soldiers, Marius </cover>recruited poor soldiers with promises of pay and land. To serve as a military leader against Rome's enemies, Marius was elected consul a record-breaking seven times. Alaric the Visigoth, Who Sacked Rome Charles Phelps Cushing / ClassicStock / Getty Images The Visigoth king Alaric was told he would conquer Rome, but his troops treated the imperial capital with notable tenderness—they spared Christian churches, thousands of souls who sought refuge therein, and burned relatively few buildings. His demands of the Senate included freedom for 40,000 enslaved Goths.\" , \"start_char\": 1905."}}
