{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ marius mules roman army reforms)"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1567251276#16_3198750978","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1888","rank":1,"snippet":"\"end_char\": 8976.000000 , \"headings\": \"What were the impact of the military reforms of Marius on Rome? What were the impact of the military reforms of Marius on Rome? Background The career of Marius Marius Reforms The social and political impact of the Marian <cover>Reforms Impact on the military Conclusion References \" , \"segment\": \"The century was divided into sub-units that contained eight legionaries and two non-combatant support staff. The Roman legionnaires would eat, fight, and live together, which created a great spirit de corps. Marius insisted on regular training and drills, and this meant that the Romans were always physically fit. He also ordered that every man carrying his gear and equipment and the soldiers, as a result, referred to themselves as ‘Marius’ Mules.’ This meant that the army </cover>did not have lengthy supply lines and massive baggage trains but was very mobile and flexible. Marius believed that morale was essential in the military, and he offered the ordinary soldiers and men retirement benefits. These were usually in the form of land for the common soldier and money for officers. Upon retirement, a "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"36/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1567251276#16_3198750978","relevance":3,"reason":"The result is from a document dedicated to the impact of Marius' military reforms and directly states specific changes he made: regular training and drills, requiring soldiers to carry their own gear ('Marius' Mules') to make the army more mobile, and providing retirement benefits such as land or money.","learned":"Beyond recruitment, Marius changed the Roman army's organization and logistics by training soldiers regularly, making them carry equipment to reduce baggage trains, improving mobility, and offering veteran benefits."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1888","reason":"The Marius' Mules query is producing a direct grade-3 segment from a dedicated reform article; continue to find more evidence about logistical and retirement-benefit changes."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1888"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_960272640#3_1707193337","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1888","rank":2,"snippet":"master? relating to or involving money \" , \"segment\": \"Freebase (0.00 / 0 votes) Rate this definition: Sarcina Sarcina is a Latin word meaning a marching pack as carried by Roman Legionaries, the heavy infantry of the Roman legions. Most of a legionary's equipment other than his arms and armour would, in early times, have been consigned to a baggage train and borne by <cover>mules and carts. Image of pack donkey on a Greek wine cup circa 480 BC, Museum of Fine Arts, Boston. However, following the reforms of the Roman General Gaius Marius, the soldiers were expected to carry much of their rations and equipment themselves. This was done to reduce the size of the baggage train and increase the mobility of the army </cover>by allowing the soldiers to move strategically independently of the train. Such was the load of the soldiers that they became known as Marius' Mules. The appearance of the marching pack is known from illustrations on Trajan's Column. Here it can be seen that a legionary's sarcina was carried on a pole called a furca and would have included: ⁕Loculus - a satchel ⁕Cloak bag ⁕Cooking pot ⁕Patera - mess "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"37/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_960272640#3_1707193337","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly supports one aspect of Marius' changes by saying that after Gaius Marius' reforms Roman soldiers carried much of their own rations and equipment, reducing baggage trains and increasing army mobility, but the document is a definition of 'sarcina' rather than dedicated to Marius' reforms.","learned":"The 'Marius' Mules' reform made legionaries carry more of their own gear and supplies, reducing baggage trains and making the Roman army more mobile."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1888","reason":"The Marius' Mules query is yielding direct evidence about a specific military/logistical reform; continue for possible dedicated reform documents or additional direct snippets."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1888"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1567251276#15_3198749385","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1888","rank":3,"snippet":"legion was six thousand, and of these, 4800 would be legionnaires and the rest support staff, mainly servants. Marius wanted every legion to be a self-contained fighting force. The legion was divided into centuries commanded by a centurion. Each century was comprised of 80 soldiers and twenty support staff. The century was divided into sub-units that contained eight legionaries and two non-combatant support staff. The <cover>Roman legionnaires would eat, fight, and live together, which created a great spirit de corps. Marius insisted on regular training and drills, and this meant that the Romans were always physically fit. He also ordered that every man carrying his gear and equipment and the soldiers, as a result, referred to themselves as ‘Marius’ Mules.’ This meant that the army did not have lengthy supply lines and massive baggage trains but was very mobile and flexible.\" , \"start_char\": 7495.000000 , \"title\": \"What were the impact of the military reforms </cover>of Marius on Rome? - DailyHistory.org\" , \"url\": \"https://dailyhistory.org/What_were_the_impact_of_the_military_reforms_of_Marius_on_Rome%3F\" }"}}
