{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"gaius marius\" \"reforms to the military\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1019268283#11_1122812741","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1892","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1019268283#11_1122812741\" , \"end_char\": 10000.000000 , \"headings\": \"Gaius Marius<cover>Gaius Marius Contents Life Early career Subordinate to Metellus Run for the consulship War in Numidia Cimbri and Teutones As consul Reforms to the military</cover>Battle with the Germanic tribes Sixth consulship 90s BC Social War Sulla and the First Civil War Seventh consulship and death Legacy Reforms to the legions Assemblies and foreign affairs Political violence Timeline Consulships See also Notes References Books Articles Ancient sources External links \" , \"segment\": \"Hispania Ulterior ), where he engaged in some sort of minor military operation to clear brigands from untapped mining areas. Due to his success in Spain, Marius almost certainly returned to Rome in 113 BC with his personal wealth greatly enlarged. He received no triumph on his return, but he did marry Julia, the aunt of Julius Caesar. The Julii Caesares were a patrician family, but at this period seem to have found it hard to advance beyond the praetorship into the consulship. The Julii had done so only once in the ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1019268283#0_1122787096","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_1019268283#1_1122789372","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
