{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1859"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_654642128#10_1374301143","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1859","rank":15,"snippet":"men (60 for the Triarii). Legio (Republic) - Legions in the Pre-Marian armies consisted of 60 maniples of infantry and 10 Turmae of cavalry. By 250 BC, there would be four Legions, two commanded by each Consul, two Roman legions which would be accompanied by an additional two allied legion of similar strength and structure. For every Roman Legion there would be an allied Legion Turma - a unit of cavalry in the Pre-Marian army, which usually consisted of 30 horsemen In the years following the <cover>reforms of Gaius Marius in 107 BC the organisation of the legions became standardised as follows: Contubernium - The smallest organized unit of soldiers in the Roman Army. </cover>It was composed of eight legionaries led by a non-commissioned officer called a decanus. Ten contubernia formed a centuria. Centuria - A centuria consisted of 80 men under the command of a Centurion and his Optio. Six centuria formed a cohort. Cohors (Cohortes) - A cohort consisted of 480 men.\" , \"start_char\": 6600.000000 , \"title\": \"List of Roman army unit types | Military Wiki | Fandom\" , \"url\": \"https://military.wikia.org/wiki/"}}
