{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_654642128#10_1374301143","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly supports one aspect of Marius' change by saying that after Gaius Marius' reforms in 107 BC the organization of Roman legions became standardized into units such as contubernia, centuries, and cohorts, but the document is a broad list of Roman army unit types rather than dedicated to Marius' reforms.","learned":"Marius' reforms standardized the organization of the Roman legions, including smaller units such as contubernia, centuries, and cohorts."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1859","reason":"The original direct query has produced the largest number of grade-3 and direct evidence results about Marius' army reforms; continue once more within the remaining judgment budget to seek another dedicated result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1859"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_24_654642128#9_1374299270","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1859","rank":16,"snippet":"decanus. When on the march a Legion would often march contubernium-abreast (8-abreast) and in the Imperial Legion, ten contubernia formed a centuria. Maniple (Maniplulus) - a Maniple was the Pre-Marian sub-unit of the Roman Legions, consisting of 120 men (60 for the Triarii). Legio (Republic) - Legions in the Pre-Marian armies consisted of 60 maniples of infantry and 10 Turmae of cavalry. By 250 BC, there would be four Legions, two commanded by each Consul, two Roman legions which would be accompanied by an additional two allied legion of similar strength and structure. For every Roman Legion there would be an allied Legion Turma - a unit of cavalry in the Pre-Marian army, which usually consisted of 30 horsemen In the years following the <cover>reforms of Gaius Marius in 107 BC the organisation of the legions became standardised as follows: Contubernium - The smallest organized unit of soldiers in the Roman Army.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 5878.000000 , \"title\": \"List of Roman army unit types | Military Wiki | Fandom\" , \"url\": \"https://military.wikia.org/wiki/List_of_Roman_army_unit_types\" }"}}
